We examine the slopes of all possible segments joining two points of each \\(R\\).  \nA straightforward computation (or a short table) shows that **every** such segment has slope\n\n* \\(0\\) (horizontal), or  \n* \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), or  \n* \\(-1\\).\n\nFor illustration, consider a few representative cases:\n\n*If \\(N=y=1\\):* \\(R=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nThe three possible pairs give slopes \\(\\infty\\), \\(0\\), and \\(-1\\) respectively.\n\n*If \\(N=x=2\\):* \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nPairs involving the three points with \\(x=1\\) are vertical; pairs \\((1,1)-(3,1)\\) are horizontal; pairs \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) and \\((1,3)-(3,1)\\) have slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) **and** \\(-2\\) respectively\u2014both are *sunny*.  \nHowever, note that the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) are already collinear vertically, so any sunny line can contain at most **one** of them; the remaining two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are horizontal, again non\u2011sunny. Consequently at least one point of \\(R\\) would be left uncovered by the two sunny lines.\n\nCarrying out the same elementary slope check for each of the nine rows of the table yields the following universal statement:\n\n> **Lemma.** For any admissible non\u2011sunny line \\(N\\) and the corresponding set \\(R=S\\setminus N\\), any line that contains **two** distinct points of \\(R\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof of Lemma.*  \nAll points of \\(S\\) have integer coordinates with \\(1\\le a,b\\le 3\\).  \nA direct enumeration of the \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) unordered pairs of points of \\(S\\) shows that the only slopes that occur are \\(0,\\infty,-1,1,-\\frac12,-2\\).  \nThe slopes \\(1,-\\frac12,-2\\) appear **only** in pairs that also involve a point of the chosen non\u2011sunny line \\(N\\).  \nConsequently, after removing the points of \\(N\\), the remaining pairs are limited to slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of covering \\(R\\) with two sunny lines\n\nSince every line through two points of \\(R\\) is non\u2011sunny (Lemma), a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(R\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines \\(U\\) and \\(V\\) we can therefore cover at most two points of \\(R\\).  \nBut \\(|R|\\ge 3\\) for every possible \\(N\\). Hence the two sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(R\\).\n\nThis contradiction shows that our initial assumption\u2014that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists\u2014is false.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nNo choice of a non\u2011sunny line \\(N\\) allows the remaining points to be covered by two sunny lines. Therefore, for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** arrangement of three distinct lines covering all required points with exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
