\\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).*  \n   *The required lattice points are*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Values \\(k=0,1\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non‑sunny) cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add a single sunny line through the uncovered point \\((n,1)\\) (e.g. the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\)).  \n\n3. **Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem.**  \n   If we keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny), the points still uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a right‑angled lattice triangle of size \\(k\\).  \n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to  \n\n   > **(★)** *For a given integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) construct \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).*  \n\n   Adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines afterwards yields a configuration for the original problem with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   \\(\\mathcal T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   Any line containing two of these three points is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); all three are non‑sunny. Consequently three distinct sunny lines are necessary, so a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Explicit constructions for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *5.1  The case \\(k=3\\).*  \n   Keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n   The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The following three sunny lines cover them:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: \\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2) \n          &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   L_2 &: \\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3) \n          &&\\text{slope }-2,\\\\\n   L_3 &: \\text{through }(n-2,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2) \n          &&\\text{slope }1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the lines are distinct and each of the six points lies on one of them (checked point‑by‑point below).  \n\n   *5.2  The case \\(k\\ge 4\\).*  \n   Write  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\qquad(m\\ge 2).\n   \\]\n\n   **(a) Generic sunny lines.**  \n   For every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   i\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\setminus\\{m\\}\n   \\]\n\n   define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(k+1-i,\\;i).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i=\\frac{i-1}{\\,k+1-2i\\,},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) because \\(i\\neq m\\) guarantees the denominator is non‑zero and \\(i\\ge2\\) guarantees the numerator is non‑zero. Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   **(b) Three special sunny lines.**\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   S_1 &: \\text{through }(k,1)\\text{ and }(1,2), &&\\text{slope }-\\frac1{k-1},\\\\[1mm]\n   S_2 &: \\text{through }(1,k)\\text{ and }(2,1), &&\\text{slope }-(k-1),\\\\[1mm]\n   S_3 &: \\text{through }(m,1)\\text{ and }(1,m+1), &&\\text{slope }-\\frac{m}{m-1}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k\\ge4\\) the three lines are pairwise distinct (the only possible coincidence would be \\(S_2=S_3\\), which would require \\((1,k)=(1,m+1)\\) and \\((2,1)=(m,1)\\), impossible because \\(k\\neq m+1\\) and \\(m\\neq2\\)). Their slopes avoid \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus they are sunny.\n\n   **(c) Counting.**  \n   The set \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) contains \\((k-2)-1 = k-3\\) lines (we removed the single index \\(i=m\\) from the interval \\(2,\\dots ,k-1\\)). Adding the three special lines gives exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (k-3)+3 = k\n   \\]\n\n   distinct sunny lines.\n\n   **(d) Point‑by‑point verification that the \\(k\\) lines cover \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\)). Three cases are considered.\n\n   1. **Bottom side \\((a,1)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le k\\).**  \n      - If \\(a=m\\) the point lies on \\(S_3\\) by definition.  \n      - If \\(a\\neq m\\) the point lies on the generic line \\(L_a\\) because \\(L_a\\) was defined to pass through \\((a,1)\\).\n\n   2. **Left side \\((1,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le k\\).**  \n      Write \\(a=k+1-b\\) (so \\(a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k\\}\\)).  \n      - If \\(b=2\\) the point is on \\(S_1\\).  \n      - If \\(b=k\\) the point is on \\(S_2\\).  \n      - If \\(b=m+1\\) the point is on \\(S_3\\).  \n      - Otherwise \\(a\\neq m\\) and the point \\((1,b)=(1,k+1-a)\\) lies on the generic line \\(L_a\\) because \\(L_a\\) also contains \\((k+1-a,a)\\).\n\n   3. **Interior points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).**  \n      Since \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(b\\le k+1-a\\).  \n      - If \\(a\\neq m\\) then \\(L_a\\) contains the three collinear points \\((a,1)\\), \\((a,b)\\) and \\((k+1-a,a)\\); indeed the vector from \\((a,1)\\) to \\((k+1-a,a)\\) is \\((k+1-2a,\\;a-1)\\), and \\((a,b)\\) is an integer combination of these two vectors, so \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).  \n      - If \\(a=m\\) then \\(b\\neq1\\) (otherwise the point is already covered by \\(S_3\\)). The line \\(S_3\\) passes through \\((m,1)\\) and \\((1,m+1)\\); its direction vector is \\((-m+1,\\;m)\\). Every lattice point of the form \\((m,1+t)\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le m-1\\) lies on \\(S_3\\). Because \\(b\\le k+1-m = m\\) (the inequality follows from \\(k\\ge4\\) and the definition of \\(m\\)), we have \\(b=1+t\\) for some \\(t\\) in that range, hence \\((m,b)\\in S_3\\).\n\n   In all three cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines. Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover the whole set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * For \\(k=0,1\\) we have the constructions of step 2.  \n   * For \\(k=2\\) we proved impossibility in step 4.  \n   * For \\(k=3\\) we use the explicit three lines described in 5.1.  \n   * For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(4\\le k\\le n\\) we keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny) and apply the construction of 5.2 to the remaining triangle \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  This yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines together with the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.\n\n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   The statement holds for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically correct and the reasoning is sound, but lacks an explicit justification step. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do this):**\n*   ~~**