"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).*  \n   *The required lattice points are*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Easy constructions.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non\u2011sunny) cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\), which is covered by a single sunny line (any line through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\n3. **Reduction to a triangular sub\u2011problem.**  \n   If we keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (all non\u2011sunny), the points that are still uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a right\u2011angled lattice triangle of \u201csize\u2019\u2019 \\(k\\).  \n   Consequently the original problem is equivalent to  \n\n   > **(\u2605)** *For a given integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) construct \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).*  \n\n   Adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines afterwards yields a configuration for the original problem with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k=2\\).**  \n   \\(\\mathcal T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   Any line containing two of these three points is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); all three are non\u2011sunny. Hence three distinct sunny lines are necessary, so a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility for every \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(k\\ge4\\). A sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\), unless the line is the diagonal \\(y=x\\); the diagonal contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n   *Proof of Lemma 1.*  \n   Suppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \n   Because the three points are collinear, the vectors \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_1,b_3-b_1)\\) are linearly dependent; hence the ratios  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}=m\n   \\]\n\n   are equal.  Since all coordinates are integers, \\(m\\) is a rational number.  Write \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) (\\(q>0\\)).  From the collinearity we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b_i = m a_i + c\\qquad(i=1,2,3)\n   \\]\n\n   for some integer \\(c\\).  Because each \\((a_i,b_i)\\) satisfies \\(a_i+b_i\\le k+1\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a_i + (m a_i + c) \\le k+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)a_i \\le k+1-c .\n   \\]\n\n   The left\u2011hand side is an integer for every integer \\(a_i\\); therefore \\(1+m\\) must be an integer.  Since \\(m\\) is rational, the only possibilities are \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\) or \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z-1\\).  The forbidden slopes are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); the only remaining integer slope that can occur for three lattice points inside the triangle is \\(m=1\\).  Hence any sunny line containing three points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) must be the line \\(y=x\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) together with \\(a=b\\) gives \\(2a\\le k+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Thus the diagonal contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\). \u220e\n\n   *Counting consequence.*  \n   Let a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  At most one of them can be the diagonal; the remaining \\(k-1\\) lines each cover at most two points.  Hence the total number of covered points is bounded by  \n\n   \\[\n   2(k-1)+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The cardinality of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(k\\ge4\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\;-\\;\\Bigl[2(k-1)+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr]\n   =\\frac{(k-1)(k-3)}{2}>0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the bound (1) is **strictly smaller** than the required number (2); therefore \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) cannot be covered by only \\(k\\) sunny lines when \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=3\\).**  \n   The triangle \\(\\mathcal T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: \\text{through }(3,1)\\text{ and }(1,2) &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   L_2 &: \\text{through }(2,1)\\text{ and }(1,3) &&\\text{slope }-2,\\\\\n   L_3 &: \\text{through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,2) &&\\text{slope }1,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, none has slope \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), and a direct substitution shows that each of the six points belongs to at least one of them.  Hence \\(\\mathcal T_3\\) is covered by exactly three sunny lines.\n\n7. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * For \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) we have the constructions of step\u202f2.  \n   * For \\(k=2\\) we proved impossibility (step\u202f4).  \n   * For \\(k=3\\) we use the construction of step\u202f6 together with the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); this yields a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny ones.  \n   * For every \\(k\\ge4\\) the reduction (step\u202f3) leads to the sub\u2011problem (\u2605) with the same value \\(k\\); step\u202f5 shows that (\u2605) is impossible, hence no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n   Therefore the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1\\) and \\(3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
